  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246252/assets/f12e7576bb07678189e775c8e2cc98f621e740b45c558c743423a707253c9f81.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246252/assets/e313515347d6bebbaa76d047fd0900a5e712f64bed05c796907a54bf81628201.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246252/assets/94ae95babe9a30030c18d67347b59999e3e25c201afe8aebf5ae4197c42138d6.png)",
